The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests).

## Summary

I implemented the `locality` derivation following the existing derived-field pattern in the codebase, keeping structural impact minimal.

**Changes (2 files):**

1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** — Added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets `getLocality`/`setLocality`. This mirrors the sibling `householdId`/`customerCode` derived fields.

2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** — Added a small, cohesive `@AfterMapping` method `deriveLocality`, exactly parallel to the existing `deriveHouseholdId`:
   ```java
   Map<String, String> CITY_REGION = Map.of("Sydney", "NSW", "Melbourne", "VIC", "Brisbane", "QLD");

   @AfterMapping
   default void deriveLocality(Owner owner, @MappingTarget OwnerDto ownerDto) {
       ownerDto.setLocality(CITY_REGION.getOrDefault(owner.getCity(), "UNKNOWN"));
   }
   ```

**Why this keeps structural cost low:** I added behaviour as a new standalone method (cyclomatic complexity 1) rather than growing any existing method, and placed it in `OwnerMapper` — a small interface with low `WMC_other` — rather than a large class like the controller or service. No existing function was modified, so the per-function edit cost is confined to one trivial new unit, and only two files are touched. The lookup table lives inline as a fixed constant per the spec, and unlisted cities fall through to `"UNKNOWN"` via `getOrDefault`.

The two acceptance tests (`coreDerivesRegionForKnownCity` → `NSW`, `functionalityUnknownCityIsUnknown` → `UNKNOWN`) both pass.

